Review complete — read at HEAD, nothing modified. Findings, all traced:

**(c) Implemented but wrong**

1. **canary crashes on a PASS row with no decode rate.** `canary.py:124` does arithmetic on `measured[metric]`; `verdict_line` guards the baseline side for `None` (`canary.py:143-147`) but passes `row["decode_tps"]` unguarded (`canary.py:150`). A PASS row with `decode_tps: None` is a documented state (`report.py:43-47`, `median([])` → None at `report.py:383-388`, `ttft_note` at `report.py:872-875`): a canary request arriving whole in one content delta and passing coherence makes `ohyesmlx canary --yes` abort with TypeError mid-run (`cli.py:876`, only ValueError is caught at `cli.py:865`). No test feeds a None metric (`tests/test_canary.py` uses numeric rows only).

2. **Non-LFS files are verified by size only.** `fetch.py:558` returns True after the length check when `sha256 is None`; the rationale at `fetch.py:549-551` ("a git blob oid … cannot be recomputed from the file") is false — it is `sha1("blob <len>\0"+bytes)`. `docs/interfaces.md:1401-1403` and `catalog.py:16-20` both say the carried hash "is what a download verifies". Concrete: a same-size corrupted file (bit-rot in a resumed `.incomplete`, wrong CDN body) passes `finish` (`fetch.py:822-835`) and is symlinked as verified.

3. **`catalog.py:139-153` accepts a file with both `sha256` and `git_oid`.** `docs/interfaces.md:1401` says "never both and never neither" with ValueError naming repo+field; only "never neither" is enforced. `fetch.py:211-212` rejects it later, differently.

**(b) Scope creep** — `025-02` Task 2 said "if none exists, take `--artifact-root` and report it as a finding rather than adding a resolver": `canary.py:157-177` adds `artifact_dir` (no `--artifact-root` exists, `cli.py:744-770`), and it hardcodes `~/.cache/huggingface/hub` (`canary.py:55`), ignoring `HF_HUB_CACHE` which fetch honours (`fetch.py:127-139`, 03-03 AC-2) — with `HF_HUB_CACHE` set, a fetch-downloaded model raises "fetch it before the canary runs" (`canary.py:172-176`).

**BLOCKED (one AC conflict):** 03-03 AC-7 — "Given a running download, Then a second download is refused `busy`" — vs AC-18 "two app instances cannot download the same repo at once". `fetch.py:643-645` locks per repo only, so concurrent downloads of different repos proceed (and jointly outrun the disk margin). Decide whether AC-7 means any download or the same repo.

